Step 1: What Is a Password Strength Checker?
Think of a password strength checker like a security guard at the entrance of a building. It examines your password and decides how difficult it would be for a hacker to guess or crack it. The stronger your password, the safer your accounts are from unauthorised access.

Real-World Analogy
Imagine your password is a lock on your front door:
•	A 3-digit padlock (like "abc") — Very Weak, trivially cracked
•	A basic 4-digit PIN (like "1234") — Weak
•	A deadbolt with a 6-digit combination — Fair
•	A smart lock with fingerprint recognition — Strong
•	A bank vault with multiple keys and biometric codes — Very Strong

This program acts as a security inspector: it grades your password on the above scale and tells you exactly what to improve.

What the Program Checks
The checker evaluates five security rules for every password you enter:
•	Length of at least 12 characters
•	At least one uppercase letter (A-Z)
•	At least one lowercase letter (a-z)
•	At least one digit (0-9)
•	At least one special character (!, @, #, $, etc.)

It also cross-references the password against a built-in list of the most commonly guessed passwords that hackers try first.

Step 2: Breaking the Problem Into Sub-Tasks
Before writing any code, it helps to think about what the program needs to do, step by step:

Sub-Task 1: Get Input from the User
•	Prompt the user to type a password (hidden for security using getpass)
•	Allow repeated attempts in a loop until the user presses Enter to quit

Sub-Task 2: Evaluate Each Security Rule
For each password entered, check all five rules using regular expressions and length comparison.

Sub-Task 3: Detect Common Passwords
Convert the password to lowercase and check whether it appears in the COMMON_PASSWORDS list. If it does, flag it as Very Weak regardless of other results.

Sub-Task 4: Calculate the Strength Score
Count how many of the five rules pass. Map the count to a label (Very Weak through Very Strong) and a numeric score (1-5).

Sub-Task 5: Generate Personalised Tips
For every rule the password fails, add a friendly, specific tip explaining what to fix.

Sub-Task 6: Display the Results
Print a clean terminal report with a visual progress bar, a checklist of all five rules, and the list of tips.

Step 3: Writing the Actual Code
Here is a line-by-line walkthrough of every important part of password_complexity_checker.py.

Imports and Setup
import re
import getpass

re — Python’s built-in regular expression library, used to search for patterns inside strings (e.g. 'does this contain a digit?').
getpass — Hides the password as the user types it, exactly like a real login form. Without this, the typed text would be visible on screen.

The Common Passwords List
COMMON_PASSWORDS = [
    "password", "123456", "qwerty", "letmein",
    "admin", "iloveyou", "welcome", "monkey"
]

This constant holds passwords that are so frequently used that hackers always try them first. If the user's password (lowercased) matches any entry, it is automatically rated Very Weak — no matter how many rules it passes.

The check_password() Function
def check_password(password):
This is the core logic of the program. It accepts one argument (the password string) and returns a dictionary containing the full analysis.

Rule Detection
is_long_enough = len(password) >= 12
has_uppercase  = bool(re.search(r"[A-Z]", password))
has_lowercase  = bool(re.search(r"[a-z]", password))
has_number     = bool(re.search(r"[0-9]", password))
has_symbol     = bool(re.search(r"[^A-Za-z0-9]", password))
is_common      = password.lower() in COMMON_PASSWORDS

re.search(pattern, string) scans the whole string for the pattern. It returns a match object (truthy) or None (falsy). Wrapping it in bool() gives us a clean True or False.

The pattern [^A-Za-z0-9] means 'any character that is NOT a letter or digit' — capturing all symbols and punctuation.

Scoring and Labelling
rules  = [is_long_enough, has_uppercase, has_lowercase, has_number, has_symbol]
passed = sum(rules)

Python evaluates True as 1 and False as 0, so sum(rules) counts exactly how many of the five rules were satisfied.

if is_common:          strength, score = "Very Weak", 1
elif passed == 5:      strength, score = "Very Strong", 5
elif passed == 4:      strength, score = "Strong", 4
elif passed == 3:      strength, score = "Fair", 3
elif passed == 2:      strength, score = "Weak", 2
else:                  strength, score = "Very Weak", 1

The common-password check is placed first as an override so that even a technically complex common password is rated Very Weak.

Generating Tips
tips = []
if is_common:      tips.append("This is a very common password...")
if not is_long_enough: tips.append("Make it longer — at least 12 characters.")
# ... one tip per failed rule
if not tips:       tips.append("Excellent! Save this in a password manager.")

Each failed rule appends one specific, actionable suggestion. If all rules pass, a congratulatory message is shown instead.

The show_results() Function
def show_results(result):
    bars = {1:"[=         ]", 2:"[===       ]", 3:"[=====     ]",
            4:"[=======   ]", 5:"[==========]"}

This function receives the result dictionary and prints a formatted terminal report. The bars dictionary maps each score to a visual ASCII progress bar, giving users instant graphical feedback.

The Main Loop
if __name__ == "__main__":
    while True:
        password = getpass.getpass("  Enter a password (or press Enter to quit): ")
        if not password:
            break
        show_results(check_password(password))

The while True: loop keeps the program running until the user presses Enter with an empty input. getpass.getpass() handles hidden input at the operating system level — not just visually masked — making it suitable for real security utilities.

